3.47.827.255 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"In the context of operating systems and concurrent programming, **processes** and **threads** are fundamental units of execution, but they differ significantly in terms of resource usage, communication, and performance. Here's a breakdown of their differences and when to choose one over the other in a **web server** context.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 **Process**\nA **process** is an independent program execution instance with its own:\n- Memory space (code, data, heap, stack)\n- System resources (file descriptors, environment variables)\n- Execution context (registers, program counter)\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- **Isolation**: Processes do not share memory by default.\n- **Communication**: Requires **inter-process communication (IPC)** mechanisms (e.g., pipes, sockets, shared memory).\n- **Overhead**: Creating and switching between processes is relatively **expensive** in terms of CPU and memory.\n- **Stability**: A crash in one process typically doesn’t affect others.\n\n---\n\n### 🔹 **Thread**\nA **thread** is the smallest unit of execution within a process. All threads within a process share:\n- The same memory space (heap, global variables)\n"}
