3.59.221.813 D Parsed message: {"role":"assistant","content":"## Trade-offs of Adding a Message Queue Between Two Services\n\n### What It Buys You\n\n- **Decoupling.** The producer and consumer no longer need to know about each other's location, protocol, or availability. This makes it easier to modify, replace, or scale either side independently.\n- **Resilience & Fault Isolation.** If the consumer goes down, messages accumulate in the queue rather than being lost. The producer keeps working, and the system self-heals when the consumer recovers.\n- **Load Leveling / Burst Absorption.** The queue acts as a buffer that smooths out traffic spikes, letting the consumer process at its own sustainable pace without being overwhelmed.\n- **Asynchronous Processing.** The producer can fire-and-forget and immediately respond to the caller, improving latency and user experience for operations that don't need an immediate response.\n- **Scalability.** Multiple consumer instances can read from the same queue (competing consumers pattern), making it straightforward to scale out processing horizontally.\n- **Guaranteed Delivery (with persistence).** Durable queues ensure messages survive crashes, enabling at-least-once delivery semantics.\n- **Flexibility in Routing & Fan-out.** Advanced brokers support topic-based routing, content-based filtering,"}
